WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.541 --> 00:00:02.981
- Yeah and I, when I was younger,

00:00:02.981 --> 00:00:05.990
my mom had told me that she used heroin,

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:06.823
my dad used heroin.

00:00:06.823 --> 00:00:09.250
She told me, she told me and
I didn't understand it then,

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:11.210
she said over everything I would give,

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:15.390
I would give you up and your
little brother up for drugs

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:17.160
I would choose drugs over
you and your brother,

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:17.993
- [Interviewer] Your mom told you that?

00:00:17.993 --> 00:00:18.939
- Yeah and she didn't mean that

00:00:18.939 --> 00:00:21.640
to be mean or, or, or anything like that.

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:23.040
It's just the way drugs are.

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:30.100
- [Interviewer] All right, Josh.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:30.980
Josh, where'd you grow up?

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:32.340
Where are you from originally?

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:35.640
- I'm from South Jersey,
right outside of Canyon,

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:38.530
about 10 minutes from
North Philly, Kensington.

00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:40.130
- [Interviewer] So Kensington
is another community

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:41.700
similar to skid row, right?

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:43.610
- Yeah it's just about the same.

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:44.900
It was open air drug market,

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:46.350
there's a lot of homeless everywhere.

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:47.183
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:00:47.183 --> 00:00:49.794
How would you, how would
you compare the two?

00:00:49.794 --> 00:00:51.550
- There's a lot of, there
are a lot of drugs out there.

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:52.930
You know, you get a drug on any block,

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:53.950
you can't even walk down the street

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:56.700
without being offered,
whatever you want, basically.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:58.790
And honestly, it's an
oasis for drug addicts.

00:00:58.790 --> 00:00:59.623
- [Interviewer] Yeah,

00:00:59.623 --> 00:01:00.797
and the police tend to leave you alone?

00:01:00.797 --> 00:01:02.097
- Yeah they tend to leave you alone,

00:01:02.097 --> 00:01:03.470
you don't really get bothered out there.

00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:05.290
- [Interviewer] Right, yeah.

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:06.890
And how, how was your childhood?

00:01:09.377 --> 00:01:10.210
- I would say, like,

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:11.060
I didn't grow up with my mom or my pops,

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:11.920
they're both, you know,

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:13.560
both addicted to drugs,

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:14.450
they're in and out of prison.

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:16.220
So I grew up, my grandfather raised me.

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:18.430
I was getting a lot of trouble, you know,

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:23.110
on probation at a young age
and getting into foster care

00:01:24.702 --> 00:01:27.390
on like committed programs
and stuff like that.

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:28.360
Therapeutic communities.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:33.360
And in jewelry, I just acted up a lot.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:35.746
You know, got into drugs young,

00:01:35.746 --> 00:01:38.430
just like everybody else
in Jersey, you know,

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:39.950
but you know,

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:41.910
I thought it was just what
we did it's in my blood.

00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:44.130
Everybody does it, you know,
I thought it was normal.

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:45.493
- [Interviewer] How was your childhood?

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:47.780
- I'd say like--

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:49.490
- [Interviewer] Your family and all that?

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:52.440
- My grandfather raised me and my

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:54.610
it wasn't like he didn't show emotion.

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:56.980
So I wasn't like really connected to them.

00:01:56.980 --> 00:02:00.880
You know, I was more like,
I felt separate, you know?

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:02.460
- [Interviewer] So your mom
and dad didn't raise you?

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:04.063
- No, no, no.

00:02:04.063 --> 00:02:05.120
They were in a prison, you know?

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:07.534
So I didn't, I know them, you know,

00:02:07.534 --> 00:02:09.230
I just never lived with them.

00:02:09.230 --> 00:02:10.570
I never seen them together.

00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:12.130
They got locked up when
I was really young.

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:14.070
So my dad's still in prison right now,

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:16.370
but you know

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:18.350
my mom uses drugs still,

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:20.920
but I grew to knewer a
lot, know her very well.

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:24.380
You know, she's been homeless
all over the country.

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:25.213
She's taught me,

00:02:25.213 --> 00:02:26.046
she like, kinda like hippie,

00:02:26.046 --> 00:02:27.840
like just taught me how to basically

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:30.250
how to be homeless and
how to appreciate people

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:32.190
and treat people when you're homeless

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:34.160
and just how to survive.

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:35.418
- [Interviewer] So you've
been living on the streets

00:02:35.418 --> 00:02:36.443
for how long?

00:02:36.443 --> 00:02:38.720
- Been on my own since I was basically 14,

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:39.553
in and out of programs.

00:02:39.553 --> 00:02:42.040
Then 17 is when I got off of probation

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:44.110
and started traveling the
country when I was in Colorado

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:46.550
to see my moms, ended up in foster care.

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:48.590
After that I ended up
living on the streets

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:53.340
of Denver and caught a charge
right after I turned 18.

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:54.450
And I went on the run.

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:56.750
So I started working
at a carnival in Texas.

00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:58.879
My mom hooked me up with
them to basically travel

00:02:58.879 --> 00:03:01.860
with the carnival and like off papers.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:04.340
So I could, I was on the
run from the cops basically.

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:06.900
And there's like, it's like
living on the streets there.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:10.690
And we ended up back in
Denver and it's just cold.

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:11.980
It's too cold out there.

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:15.290
You know, I almost died
from just freezing to death.

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:16.370
You know, it was a foot
of snow on the ground

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:19.670
and it's like, you have to
get high, you have to drink,

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:21.300
you have to do something to stay warm.

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:23.600
You can't sleep at night
because you're shivering so bad.

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:24.433
- [Interviewer] Yeah,

00:03:24.433 --> 00:03:27.143
part of the reason I think
skid row is so populated is

00:03:27.143 --> 00:03:29.160
because it's a climate that you can deal

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:31.580
with it for a lot of these people,

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:32.600
if you can make it to L.A.

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:33.880
then you can just survive on skid row

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:36.823
and everyone including the
police leave you alone.

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:40.219
- It's like, like I said,

00:03:40.219 --> 00:03:42.620
your offered whatever you want.

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:44.470
It's not hard to find, you know, all my

00:03:44.470 --> 00:03:45.690
I know a lot of people from out

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:48.360
of state to come out here
and this is where they go.

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:52.543
You know, I come out here
every day and you know,

00:03:53.377 --> 00:03:54.778
I got like, I got a
lot of people out here.

00:03:54.778 --> 00:03:56.009
I got a lot of friends out here.

00:03:56.009 --> 00:03:58.340
- [Interviewer] What's your drug now?

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:00.730
- I do fentanyl every
day, every single day.

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:03.340
And crack, everyday, a lot of it.

00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:06.523
- [Interviewer] What do
you spend a day on it?

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:09.940
- Me and my fiance go
through about a half ounce

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:14.791
in two, three days, quarter
ounce a day, maybe of fentanyl.

00:04:14.791 --> 00:04:15.923
- [Interviewer] And that's how much?

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:18.380
- Well I mean I get pretty good prices

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:21.530
on it cause I sell drugs a little bit.

00:04:21.530 --> 00:04:23.830
So anywhere from like,

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:27.350
get it for like an eight ball for 200

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:31.083
a quarter rounds for like
anywhere from like 250,

00:04:32.247 --> 00:04:34.320
but I usually get like
a half ounce for 500,

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:37.541
600, people would try that
charges a lot more than that.

00:04:37.541 --> 00:04:40.050
But I mean it's really prevalent out here.

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:43.010
I got a pretty decent
plugs on it, but it's just,

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:44.450
it's just a lot, you know,

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:45.530
there's a lot of people that have it.

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:50.170
A lot of people that do
it, but we do too much.

00:04:50.170 --> 00:04:51.740
We do entirely too much.

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:52.573
- [Interviewer] Have you OD'd?

00:04:52.573 --> 00:04:54.700
- Yeah I've OD'd, I've OD'd
a few times in my life.

00:04:54.700 --> 00:04:56.680
First time I was a, I was 18 years old.

00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:58.850
I was doing heroin in Philly

00:04:58.850 --> 00:05:02.840
and I overdosed on that doing
shots of heroin on, on a bag.

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:04.080
Cause they'd stamped their bags out there.

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:04.930
It's called good luck.

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:06.130
It was called good luck.

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:12.180
Yeah I was in a coma after
that one, went into a coma,

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:14.940
I had pneumonia in the
hospital, it nearly killed me.

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:17.210
I was in at Temple Lung Center in Philly.

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:19.160
It was really bad pneumonia after that.

00:05:20.630 --> 00:05:23.400
You know, I've tried getting sober a lot.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:24.280
A lot of times I came

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:26.760
out here to California to go to rehab,

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:28.550
just like you know a lot of other people

00:05:28.550 --> 00:05:30.140
that are from out of state

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:31.520
you know, it's just--

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:32.650
- [Interviewer] Is that
what brought you here?

00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:33.503
- Yeah yeah.

00:05:33.503 --> 00:05:34.336
That's what brought me here.

00:05:34.336 --> 00:05:35.210
I know a lot of people out
here that came out here

00:05:35.210 --> 00:05:39.770
for that and I really wanted
to be sober a lot of times.

00:05:39.770 --> 00:05:41.550
I really thought like that was the answer

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:45.023
but it is, you know, it was
always with all respect like,

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:47.890
eventually that has to come.

00:05:47.890 --> 00:05:49.370
But right now I've been doing

00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:51.860
doing fentanyl every day
for more than a year.

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:52.890
And I'm actually scared.

00:05:52.890 --> 00:05:56.670
Yeah last night was the first
time I've been been without

00:05:56.670 --> 00:05:57.900
for as long as I've been without.

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:00.620
And it was like that I've been

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:03.242
able to get it every day, you know?

00:06:03.242 --> 00:06:06.273
I was so sick last night.

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:08.550
- [Interviewer] But you
couldn't get it last night

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:09.610
